<chapter xml:id="ch-data-frames-meta">
  <title>Data Frames Meta</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This section dives into the features of the package <c>DataFramesMeta</c> which provides a simpler interface to
      many of the built-in features of the <c>DataFrames</c> package.
      In most cases the <c>DataFramesMeta</c> package provides macros with the same name as the <c>DataFrames</c>
      package.
    </p>

    <p>
      In many cases, we will repeat the same examples as in <xref ref="ch-manipulating-data"/>, however sometimes done
      differently.
      Additionally, this has been written to assume that you the reader have not read <xref ref="ch-manipulating-data"/>
      and this chapter stands alone.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you haven't yet, you should load the <c>DataFramesMeta</c> package with:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          using DataFramesMeta
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which also loads the <c>DataFrames</c> and <c>Chain</c> packages.
      These three packages make many data analysis relatively compact to write.
      We saw the <c>Chain</c> package in <xref ref="subsec-chain-package"/> and you should review that if you need to.
      In short, it is useful for performing a series of steps in which the previous step is passed into each subsequent
      one.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-dfm-subsetting">
    <title>Subsets of Dataframes</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        There are two ways of finding the subset of a DataFrame with <c>DataFramesMeta</c>, either finding subsets of
        columns or rows.
        Let's start with the data set:
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          data = DataFrame(
              A = 1:2:13,
              B = ["M", "F", "F", "M", "X", "F", "M"],
              C = [3.0, 2.5, pi, -2.3, 1/3, 56, 100],
              D = [(-1)^n//n for n=1:7]
          )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        which will give a dataset with 7 rows of date and each column is a different data type.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-dfm-select">
      <title>Subsets of Columns with <c>@select</c></title>

      <p>
        Selecting columns (or finding a subset of columns) is performed with the <c>@select</c> macro.
        Again, we will wrap all commands in a <c>@chain</c> block to make it easier later with more complex cases.
        We can select only columns <c>A</c> and <c>D</c> with
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          @chain data begin
            @select(:A, :D)
          end
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        will return a DataFrame with just the A and D columns or
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>7×2 DataFrame</cline>
          <cline>  Row  A      D</cline>
          <cline>       Int64  Rational…  </cline>
          <cline>  1    1      -1//1</cline>
          <cline>  2    3      1//2</cline>
          <cline>  3    5      -1//3</cline>
          <cline>  4    7      1//4</cline>
          <cline>  5    9      -1//5</cline>
          <cline>  6    11     1//6</cline>
          <cline>  7    13     -1//7</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        We can use <c>@select(Not(:B))</c> to get all columns except <c>B</c> or <c>@select(Between(:B,:D))</c> will
        return all columns between and including <c>B</c> and <c>D</c>.
        <fn>If the <c>@select</c> is used only for selecting columns, the commands <c>select(df,col1, col2)</c> and
        <c>@select(df,col1, col2)</c> give the same results, where <c>select</c> was seen in <xref ref="ch-manipulating-data"/></fn>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-dfm-subset-rows">
      <title>Finding the subset of rows with <c>@subset</c></title>

      <p>
        The <c>@subset</c> macro will return a DataFrame with some subset of the rows <mdash/> which could include all
        or none of the rows.
        The following command will return all of the rows in the DataFrame <c>data</c> where values in the <c>A</c>
        column are less than 10:
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          @chain data begin
             @subset(:A .&lt; 10)
          end
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        This
        <fn>If you read <xref ref="ch-manipulating-data"/>, this was done with the command: <cd>
              <cline>@chain data begin</cline>
            <cline>  subset(:A =&gt; a-&gt; a .&lt; 10)</cline>
          <cline>end</cline>
        </cd>
      which is quite a bit more complicated. 
    </fn>
        returns
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>5×4 DataFrame</cline>
          <cline>  Row  A      B       C         D</cline>
          <cline>       Int64  String  Float64   Rational…</cline>
          <cline>  1    1      M       3.0       -1//1</cline>
          <cline>  2    3      F       2.5       1//2</cline>
          <cline>  3    5      F       3.14159   -1//3</cline>
          <cline>  4    7      M       -2.3      1//4</cline>
          <cline>  5    9      X       0.333333  -1//5</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        The argument in the <c>@select</c> macro is an expression that is an array of booleans.
        The inside is a shorthand way to write:
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          data.A .&lt; 10
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        which returns the vector <c>BitVector[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0]</c>.
        Recall that the <c>.</c> in front of the <c>&lt;</c> is a operator on vectors.
        This is called <em>broadcasting</em> and covered in <xref ref="sect-broadcasting"/>.
        Applying <c>@subset</c> to the vector of booleans returns the top five rows of <c>data</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Other subsetting is simpler as well.
        If you want the rows in which the product of columns <c>C</c> and <c>D</c>, then
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          @chain data begin
            @subset(:C .* :D .&gt; 2)
          end
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        will accomplish this and return
      </p>

      <p>
        <cd>
          <cline>5×4 DataFrame</cline>
          <cline>  Row  A      B       C         D</cline>
          <cline>       Int64  String  Float64   Rational…</cline>
          <cline>  1    11     F       56.0      1//6</cline>
        </cd>
      </p>

      <p>
        And if the dot notation is still confusing then applying the <c>@.</c> in front will accomplish the same thing.
        An alternative to the above can be done with
      </p>

      <program language="julia">
        <code>
          @chain data begin
            @subset(@. :C * :D &gt; 2)
          end
        </code>
      </program>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the <c>iris</c> dataset, find all <c>setosa</c> irises with <c>SepalWidth</c> larger than 4 using the
            <c>@subset</c> command.
            How many are in the dataset?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-dfm-transforms">
    <title>Transforming DataFrames</title>

    <p>
      Often, we need to perform a calculation on the columns of a DataFrame.
      There are two ways to accomplish this, using <c>@transform</c> to add a new column to the DataFrame or using
      <c>@select</c> to take a subset of the existing columns.
    </p>

    <p>
      As an example of using <c>@transform</c>, we will start with the <c>data</c> DataFrame and create a column that is
      the square of the <c>A</c> column.
      This is accomplished with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @chain data begin
            @transform(:Asq = :A.^2)
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and the result is
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×5 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row  A      B       C         D          Asq</cline>
        <cline>     Int64  String  Float64   Rational…  Int64</cline>
        <cline>1    1      M       3.0       -1//1      1</cline>
        <cline>2    3      F       2.5       1//2       9</cline>
        <cline>3    5      F       3.14159   -1//3      25</cline>
        <cline>4    7      M       -2.3      1//4       49</cline>
        <cline>5    9      X       0.333333  -1//5      81</cline>
        <cline>6    11     F       56.0      1//6       121</cline>
        <cline>7    13     M       100.0     -1//7      169</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      If instead, we only want the resulting DataFrame to have the new column, we can use a <c>@select</c>.
      For example,
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @chain data begin
            @select(:Asq = :A.^2)
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      resulting in
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×5 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row  Asq</cline>
        <cline>     Int64</cline>
        <cline>1    1</cline>
        <cline>2    9</cline>
        <cline>3    25</cline>
        <cline>4    49</cline>
        <cline>5    81</cline>
        <cline>6    121</cline>
        <cline>7    169</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      The <c>@select</c> can be a combination of subsetting columns as above as well as functions that produce new
      columns.
      Let's say that we want columns <c>A</c> and <c>B</c> from the original DataFrame as well as the square of the
      <c>A</c> column.
      This is done with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @chain data begin
          @select(:A, :B, :Asq = :A.^2)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      resulting in
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×3 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row   A       B        Asq</cline>
        <cline>      Int64   String   Int64</cline>
        <cline>1     1       M        1</cline>
        <cline>2     3       F        9</cline>
        <cline>3     5       F        25</cline>
        <cline>4     7       M        49</cline>
        <cline>5     9       X        81</cline>
        <cline>6     11      F        121</cline>
        <cline>7     13      M        169</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      Both <c>@select</c> and <c>@transform</c> can use multiple columns.
      The following example, takes the product between columns <c>C</c> and <c>D</c>.
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
				@chain data begin
          @select(:prod = :C .* :D)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      This returns
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×1 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>  Row  prod</cline>
        <cline>       Float64</cline>
        <cline>  1    -3.0</cline>
        <cline>  2    1.25</cline>
        <cline>  3    -1.0472</cline>
        <cline>  4    -0.575</cline>
        <cline>  5    -0.0666667</cline>
        <cline>  6    9.33333</cline>
        <cline>  7    -14.2857</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      Note: there are also commands <c>@select!</c> and <c>@transform!</c> that perform the operations within the given
      dataframe.
      These are generally destructive in that each overwrites the previous DataFrame but can save a lot of computational
      time for large DataFrames.
      It is recommended to stay with the non mutating (without the !) versions unless you need to save time/memory.
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <p>
        Using the <c>iris</c> dataframe produce a new column called <c>area</c> which is the area of a petal using the
        <c>PetalLength</c> and <c>PetalWidth</c> variables and the area of an ellipse.
        Use the <c>@transform</c> command.
      </p>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-dfm-sorting">
    <title>Sorting a DataFrame with <c>@orderby</c></title>

    <p>
      It's common to want to order the results of a DataFrame.
      In <c>DataFramesMeta</c>, this is done with the <c>@orderby</c> command.
      <fn>Note: the command within the <c>DataFrames</c> package is <c>sort</c>, but is not as flexible as <c>@orderby</c>.</fn>
    </p>

    <p>
      If we want to sort the <c>data</c> DataFrame by the <c>C</c> column, we can either use the inline command
      <c>@orderby(data,:C)</c> or within the <c>@chain</c> block with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @chain data begin
          @orderby(:C)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and the results are
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×4 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>  Row  A      B       C         D</cline>
        <cline>       Int64  String  Float64   Rational…</cline>
        <cline>  1    7      M       -2.3      1//4</cline>
        <cline>  2    9      X       0.333333  -1//5</cline>
        <cline>  3    3      F       2.5       1//2</cline>
        <cline>  4    1      M       3.0       -1//1</cline>
        <cline>  5    5      F       3.14159   -1//3</cline>
        <cline>  6    11     F       56.0      1//6</cline>
        <cline>  7    13     M       100.0     -1//7</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      and notice that although we didn't specify, <c>@orderby</c> sorts numerically in increasing order.
      If we sort by another data type, then the sorting may be different.
      For example, if we sort by column <c>B</c> with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          @chain data begin
            @orderby(:B)
          end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      results in
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×4 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row   A       B        C          D</cline>
        <cline>      Int64   String   Float64    Rational…</cline>
        <cline>1     3       F        2.5        1//2</cline>
        <cline>2     5       F        3.14159   -1//3</cline>
        <cline>3     11      F        56.0       1//6</cline>
        <cline>4     1       M        3.0       -1//1</cline>
        <cline>5     7       M       -2.3        1//4</cline>
        <cline>6     13      M        100.0     -1//7</cline>
        <cline>7     9       X        0.333333  -1//5</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      and the DataFrame has be ordered <em>lexigraphically</em> because column <c>B</c> is a string.
      If we would like the resulting DataFrame above to next be sorted by column <c>C</c>, then we change the
      <c>@orderby</c> to <c>@orderby(:B, :C)</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If instead, we want to order by a function of a column, then we can do that.
      This is how one would sort in a descending order.
      The code:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        @chain data begin
          @orderby(-:C)
        end
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      produces
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>7×4 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row   A       B        C   D</cline>
        <cline>      Int64   String   Float64    Rational…</cline>
        <cline>1     13      M        100.0      -1//7</cline>
        <cline>2     11      F        56.0        1//6</cline>
        <cline>3     5       F        3.14159    -1//3</cline>
        <cline>4     1       M        3.0        -1//1</cline>
        <cline>5     3       F        2.5         1//2</cline>
        <cline>6     9       X        0.333333   -1//5</cline>
        <cline>7     7       M       -2.3         1//4</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sort the <c>data</c> DataFrame by column <c>A</c> mod 3.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          Put column <c>A</c> mod 3 in the <c>@orderby</c> and don't forget to handle the broadcasting.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Joining DataFrames</title>

    <p>
      Often, data is split between different tables (or files) and they need to be joined before doing analysis.
      <fn>The functions in this section are not from the <c>DataFramesMeta</c>, but from the <c>DataFrames</c> package.  
        If you already have read <xref ref="ch-manipulating-data"/>, then you can skip this section.  
        There is nothing new presented here.</fn>
      Let's revisit the dataset from <xref ref="ch-data"/>:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
				simpsons = DataFrame(
				  name=["Homer","Marge","Lisa","Bart","Maggie","Apu","Moe", "Milhouse", "Patty"],
				  age =[45,42,8,10,1,38,59, 10, 46],
				  current_school_grade = [missing, missing, 2, 4, missing, missing, missing, 4, missing],
				  favorite_food = ["pork chops","casserole","salad","hamburger",missing,"saag paneer","peanuts", missing, "Lady Laramie 100s"]
				)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and a second dataset
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
				jobs = DataFrame(
				  name = ["Homer","Marge","Apu","Moe", "Wiggam"],
				  job = ["nuclear technician", "housewife", "store owner", "bartender", "police chief"],
				  salary = [50_000, 25_000, 60_000, 15_000, 75_000]
				)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and we want to join these two together.
      In order to join two datasets, we need to know which column is common to both datasets.
      This typically occurs when there is common data between the two datasets often with the same column name, but not
      necessarily.
      In this case, the column that makes most sense is the <c>name</c> column, which is common to both.
      For this example, we'll use the <c>innerjoin</c> command and the keyword attributed <c>on</c> with the common
      column name.
      If we perform the following:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
				innerjoin(simpsons, jobs, on = :name)
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and the result will be
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>5×6 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>Row  name    age     current_school_grade  favorite_food     job                  salary</cline>
        <cline>     String  Int64   Int64?                String?           String               Int64</cline>
        <cline>1    Homer   45      missing               pork chops        nuclear technician   50000</cline>
        <cline>2    Marge   42      missing               casserole         housewife            25000</cline>
        <cline>3    Apu     38      missing               saag              paneer  store owner  60000</cline>
        <cline>4    Moe     59      missing               peanuts           bartender            15000</cline>
        <cline>5    Patty   46      missing               Lady Laramie 100s missing              missing</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      The column that the sets are joined on are called the <em>key</em> of the DataFrame.
      The <c>innerjoin</c> command returns all rows in which the key is common to both datasets.
      This can be thought of as the intersection of the DataFrames and this is the most common joins.
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        If the column names with the values that you wish to join by are in different column names, you should use the
        option <c> on = col1 =&gt; col2 </c>, where <c>col1</c> is in the first DataFrame and <c>col2</c> in the second
        one.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>
      You may be surprised that there are only 5 rows in this DataFrame, since there were 9 rows in the first DataFrame.
      The command <c>innerjoin</c> merges two DataFrames, by including only where the <c>name</c> column is in both of
      the two DataFrames.
      There are other ways to join Dataframes.
      See <xref ref="sect-dataframe-joins"/> and
      <url href="https://dataframes.juliadata.org/stable/lib/functions/#Joining">the DataFrames documentation on joins</url>
      for a list of the
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the <c>outerjoin</c> command on the same two DataFrames as above, and discuss the similarities and
          differences with the <c>innerjoin</c> command.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          If we evaluate:
        </p>

        <program language="julia">
          <code>
					outerjoin(simpsons, jobs, on = :name)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          then the result is
        </p>

        <p>
          <cd>
            <cline>9×6 DataFrame</cline>
            <cline>Row  name      age      current_school_grade  favorite_food      job                 salary</cline>
            <cline>     String    Int64?   Int64?                String?            String?             Int64?</cline>
            <cline>1    Homer     45       missing               pork chops         nuclear technician  50000</cline>
            <cline>2    Marge     42       missing               casserole          housewife           25000</cline>
            <cline>3    Apu       38       missing               saag paneer        store owner         60000</cline>
            <cline>4    Moe       59       missing               peanuts            bartender           15000</cline>
            <cline>5    Patty     46       missing               Lady Laramie 100s	missing             missing</cline>
            <cline>6    Lisa      8        2                     salad              missing             missing</cline>
            <cline>7    Bart      10       4                     hamburger          missing             missing</cline>
            <cline>8    Maggie    1        missing               missing            missing             missing</cline>
            <cline>9    Milhouse  10       4                     missing            missing             missing</cline>
            <cline>10   Wiggam    missing  missing               missing            police chief        75000</cline>
          </cd>
        </p>

        <p>
          Note that this time there are 10 rows because there are a total of 10 separate values for the <c>key</c> or in
          this case the <c>name</c> column.
          There are still the same number of columns, one of each which comes from the two DataFrames.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Summarizing Data</title>

    <p>
      Generally one wants to summarize a large dataset to a few numbers to get a sense or for comparison purposes.
      The <c>describe</c> function from <c>DataFrames</c> does this, but in a fixed way.
      A more general function to do this is called the <c>@combine</c> function.
      Consider the following and note that the <c>Statistics</c> package has been loaded for the <c>mean</c> and
      <c>std</c> functions.
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        using Statistics
        @combine(data, :mean_C = mean(:C), :mean_D = mean(:D), :std_C = std(:C), :n = length(:A))
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      which returns
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>1×4 DataFrame</cline>
        <cline>  Row  mean_C    mean_D      std_C    n</cline>
        <cline>       Float64   Rational…   Float64  Int64</cline>
        <cline>1      23.2393   -319//2940  39.5518  7</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      And this returns a new dataframe with 1 row and 4 columns (mean of C, mean of D, standard deviation of C and the
      number of rows in C).
    </p>

    <note>
      <p>
        If the command <c>@combine</c> is used on a DataFrame, then the result is a DataFrame with a single row.
        The number of columns is the total number of functions applied in the <c>combine</c> command.
      </p>

      <p>
        We will also use combine after splitting a dataset for comparison purposes.
        We'll do this below in <xref ref="sect-split-apply-combine"/> in which there are multiply rows, each of one for
        the way that the original is split.
      </p>
    </note>
  </section>
</chapter>